.TH SIZE 1
.SH NAME
size \- size of an object file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B size
[\ \-o\ ] [\ \-x\ ] [\ file\ ...\ ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B size
prints the sizes in bytes of the text (code), the initialized data and
the bss (uninitialized data) of each
.IR file ,
an executable program, an object file, or each member of a library,
and their sum:
.PP
.nf
	$ size prog
	7888 + 384 + 48 = 8320
.fi
.PP
When more than one file is named, each line begins with the file's
name; a library member is shown as library(member). With no
.IR file ,
size shows a.out.
.PP
For an object file, the bss is the space asked for by its common and
local uninitialized variables. For a program, the sum does not include
the stack, whose size
.B "cc \-F"
sets, or what it takes with
.BR malloc (3).
On the 8086 a program's text must fit in 64K, and its data, bss, stack
and allocated memory together in another 64K.
.PP
The options are:
.TP 6
.B \-o
Print the sizes in octal.
.TP
.B \-x
Print the sizes in hexadecimal.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is 1 if a file could not be read, and 0 otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
cc(1), nm(1), strip(1), ar86(1)
.SH HISTORY
A
.B size
command appeared in Version 3 AT&T UNIX.
.SH AUTHORS
This size was written for the xenix86-devsys Development System.
